<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maubeuge</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maubeuge is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a> in the Nord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, formerly part of the County of Hainaut. It grew around a seventh-century convent founded by Saint Aldegund and became a cloth town. France gained it by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> in 1678, and Vauban fortified it. Besieged in 1793 and in 1914, it remains an industrial town near the Belgian border.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maubeuge.jpg" alt="Maubeuge" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Chatsam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Porte_de_mons_maubeuge.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Maubeuge is a commune in the Nord department of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. It lies on both banks of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sambre">Sambre</a>, which is canalised at this point, 36 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> and about 9 km from the Belgian border. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> name was Malbodium, from the Old Frankish Malboden, meaning assizes of Boden. Because of its position near the border, the railway station has lines towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and Charleroi in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The town owes its origin to Maubeuge Abbey, a double monastery for men and women founded in the 7th century, whose founder's relics are kept in the church. The area later belonged to the territory of Hainaut. As part of the Spanish Netherlands the town changed hands several times before it was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaties-of-nijmegen">Treaty of Nijmegen</a> of 1678. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a> had it extensively fortified as part of Vauban's pré carré, a plan that protected the northern border of France with a double line of fortresses.</p><p>In 1793 Prince Josias of Saxe-Coburg-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saalfeld">Saalfeld</a> besieged Maubeuge, and the siege was lifted by the victory of Wattignies, which a monument in the town commemorates. An attempt on the town in 1814 failed, but it capitulated after vigorous resistance during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days</a>. After the War of 1870 a new fortress was built, later modernised and enlarged, with an entrenched camp of 18 miles in perimeter. Its forts were besieged by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-empire">German Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a> bombardments in May 1940 destroyed 90 per cent of the town centre, and fighting occurred again around its outskirts in early September 1944, involving units of the U.S. 1st Infantry Division.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town was rebuilt after the war, and the new Hôtel de Ville was completed in 1970. Maubeuge has foundries, forges and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/blast-furnace">blast furnaces</a>, together with manufacture of machine tools and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/porcelain">porcelain</a>. It was the early home of the cyclist Maurice Garin, winner of the first Tour de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1903, who began his career with the local cycling club in 1892.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maubeuge” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the twelfth century towns grew on trade and crafts: Valenciennes, the largest, and <b>Maubeuge</b> produced woollen cloth for regional and international markets, while Mons became the seat of government.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The victory allowed the French to raise the siege of <b>Maubeuge</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-16">16 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
